  europe and the rest of us because we  think that germany and europe often pick  these issues with countries they want  but are not consistent in picking the  issues with everybody so there's a lack  of consistency on this  um lack of consistency minister pandor  you are full of praise for for angela  merkel but did you see a lack of  consistency when it came to german  policies towards south africa as well  are you entirely  happy well there there are aspects as i  said that are of concern uh firstly the  whole matter of reparations in fact it's  not just germany's europe itself  in fact many european countries  boycotted our recent commemoration of  the  durban declaration on racism and other  intolerances at the u.n where we had a  roundtable so that just illustrates the  attitude of some leaders toward the  issue of black people  and racism  in the in the global community but i  think uh  leaders have to represent their  countries as well and i understand that  uh fully  but i i think there were times where she  went against even the german grain  um you know i am a fan because there are  very few women  uh who would uh are leaders of her  stature and who would stand up on many  uh  very issues that impact on the world  community and speaking from the south  african perspective in africa as well  she's been part of defining a new  strategy between the european union and  the african union and this is a very  important strategy and as i said she has  encouraged foreign direct investment  into africa for key  development initiatives on the continent  and many african countries are  benefiting from the work in the g20  compact uh with africa from the  perspective of south africa she has  supported the training of young people  in technical and artisanal skills an  area that we need a great deal of  training for young people in  so um thank you minister for that  because we don't have a whole lot of  time left i i need to get in there  because there's a of course one topic we  still need to discuss and it sort of  resonates with what you just said going  against the german grain may be even or  going against the grain of the european  union mr balofakis were you surprised at  what  angela marco did during the migration  crisis in 2015.  i was very pleasantly surprised  when she let the syrians in  and as i said before i applauded and i  i felt moved by her humanitarianism but  unfortunately only lasted two weeks when  they said they would the christian  democratic party started  boiling over with anger at her she  immediately reversed course and she went  from  wonderful humanitarianism to  a kind of  cynical misanthropy the agreement with  turkey  effectively meant  that the european union  under basis america's guidance was  sending billions of euros to the  president of turkey as a bribe so that  he would allow us europeans  to violate international law regarding  refugees effectively to deny refugees  their right  to uh  seek asylum in europe uh  you know the maurya  prison camp in lesbos is forever going  to haunt mrs merkel's legacy and that  comes you know this is why i'm saying  this is of course not officially called  a prison camp at a but a refugee camp  but yes understood what you mean no i'm  sorry  it was a prison camp you have not been  there i have been there it was a prison  camp and now it's even more of a prison  camp now in on other islands too there  are huge wars  you know somebody from nigeria somebody  from ghana somebody from afghanistan  they try to seek asylum and they're  imprisoned this is a prison camp we are  building within europe as part of a  policy that clashes  remarkably and awfully with merkel's  two-week  dose of extreme humanitarianism is for  which you know i will love her forever  right and is that is that um i'm sure  she'd be happy to hear that is she um is  that the defining moment of her  chancellory if you had to sum it up in a  couple of quick words mr [ __ ] and  i'll put the same question to all our  other panelists too  what is the defining moment of her  legacy  february 2009  she's told that the german banks have  gone kaput  and she gives them a huge bailout of 406  billion  euros without getting rid of the bankers  effectively practicing socialism for the  bankers and austerity for the rest of  europe that erect our chance of a  genuine democratic political union in  the european union  and ambassador singh what is the  defining moment  briefly  for you in angela merkel's 16 years in  office  you know when you talk about the refugee  crisis  you know in some people's life there's  an aha moment this was an oops moment  so i was not impressed by but what i was  impressed with was the  support and backing she gave to the g20  now i and the greek minister look at it  differently because we are not a  neighbor of germany we are not in the eu  we are looking at it from outside like  minister pandora is so i think the g20  has been something great for  all of us and i think merkel played a  very important role in that  thank you very much ambassador same  question to you minister pandor in those  16 years what was the defining moment of  angela merkel for you  i would agree with ambassador singh on  the g20 but i think we can't pull her  down  on the decision to  accept refugees into germany when  everybody was pushing back  there are many leaders in europe who did  not support her  there are many leaders in europe who  have been anti-migrants  and who have acted without respect for  human rights i don't think you could  classify angela merkel as the same as  those leaders  thank you very much minister um so i  want to thank all our  panelists for being with us today i look  back at 16 years of course uh 20 minutes  aren't nearly enough to discuss it but i  think we we got an idea of what you  think about angela merkel and i have a  feeling that even mr valuefakis will  miss her in a in a certain way if i may  say so
 

